include "../../linux.mereo"

-- REJECTED on purpose: an instance named after the scope that acquired it.
--
-- A resource is released at the end of the scope that acquired it. Its NAME was
-- not bounded the same way, so this was accepted and compiled to a
-- use-after-close followed by a double close:
--
--     _assembly_linux_close(source_descriptor);            -- the scope ended
--     n = _assembly_linux_read(source_descriptor, 0, 0);   -- ...and then this
--     _assembly_linux_close(source_descriptor);            -- ...and this
--
-- Nothing caught it. `mereoraii` did not: the second close fails with EBADF on
-- a descriptor it has already accounted for, so the audit still reported one
-- acquired and one closed.
--
-- The check needs no new bookkeeping. `live` is the set of resources still held
-- at a given step -- the planner maintains it to derive the release tower -- so
-- an owned instance that is NOT in it has already been given back. It is the
-- mirror of the `used before its construction` check standing beside it.
--
-- A name can be mentioned three ways, and each needed its own reach:
--
--   source.read (...)                          a CALL, at the receiver
--   n is source.descriptor                     a VALUE, scanned per step
--   c is adopted file (descriptor is source.descriptor)
--                                              an ADOPTION, whose bindings live
--                                              on the slot rather than the step
--
-- The last is the one that reads worst: it builds a second resource over a
-- descriptor the kernel has taken back, so the tower closes it a third time.
-- This program uses the first; `tests/blackbox.sh` pins the message they share.
--
-- What is deliberately still accepted: a scalar, a buffer, or an `already`
-- instance named after its block. None of those is released, so none goes
-- stale, and mereo does not otherwise bound a declaration to its block.
--
--   refused:  'source' was released when the scope that acquired it ended

program goes
  n is 0

  inner goes
    source is linux.file (path is "/dev/null", flags is 0, mode is 0)
    n is 1
  end

  source.read (buffer is 0, capacity is 0, count is n)

end
